[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-28", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 28 — interning is not the lever: the footprint is the index nodes", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-28.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 28 - interning is not the lever: the footprint is the index nodes, measured twice now\n\n> Seed: iteration 27's S19 - a compact representation behind the same\n> seam. First probe: are the bytes duplicate strings? Judge:\n> bench/benchmark.edn v2 (no row; a measurement).\n\n## Generate\n\n  The same ids are parsed from several CSVs (a message id from post,\n  hasCreator, replyOf, likes), so the index maps may hold several equal\n  String objects. Build the subset db as-is and with every :s and :o\n  canonicalised through one table; compare live heap (simeon, ParallelGC,\n  gc x3).\n\n  Prediction: 15-25% smaller with interning (three ~56-byte strings per\n  quad, some share of them duplicated).\n\n## Rank\n\n    as-is        588 MB   1,788,143 quads\n    interned     634 MB   828,349 distinct strings  (+46 MB: the table)\n    IC07 Datalog = index twin on the interned db: true\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Refuted. The db is not smaller with one String per distinct value;\n    the index paths already share the same String objects (the loader\n    reuses the parsed id within a CSV, and the maps hold references).\n    What remains per quad is structure: an entry in eavt, aevt and avet,\n    each a path of persistent hash-map nodes ending in a small set --\n    the ~450 B iteration 27 measured per group, now shown not to be the\n    values.\n  - So the lever is inside datalog.index: how a (subject, attribute) or\n    (attribute, value) pair holds its set of counterparts. Twenty million\n    single-element PersistentHashSets, and twenty million map entries\n    around them, are the heap. A representation that holds the common\n    single value directly and a sorted array for the many-valued case\n    changes the API's shape (`entity-attrs` returns sets) and is the\n    engine's decision: an ADR, not an iteration.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict: MEASURED, no row. Two levers eliminated (content strings,\nduplicate strings); one named (index node representation). M3 stands\nwhere iteration 27 left it, with a sharper reason.\n\nSeeds for iteration 29, in order:\n\n  - S19' (engine): a JOL-style count -- how many map nodes and set\n    objects the subset db holds per index, to put a number on the\n    singleton-set share before proposing the representation. Then the\n    ADR: a compact per-attribute index behind the same read seam.\n  - S18 (host) for the SF1-proper row with the representation as is.\n  - S15 (coverage), M2 (updates).\n"}]
